#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228931 :question/id #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-21766" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+18}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{\\left(\\frac{12}{30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c652164a-2e94-5e16-a410-5143037af134" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539463 :question-generator/id #uuid "7e061520-71a8-55d4-a2eb-a7cef8163589" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186229183.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377193} {:answer/id #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377194} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} {:answer/id #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} :answer-field/id #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377192}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98f92dde-10ee-5a2e-ae0c-afe47e1981a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336451" :question/problem "A de-icing salt reservoir can hold up to $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine with $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A stronger brine with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{\\left(\\frac{90}{90+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17fcaa1-cd0a-5b81-a422-98bed86e8456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228346} {:answer/id #uuid "cf230f49-5d88-556c-b1ef-9be0df07fa17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.7lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228347} {:answer/id #uuid "f2079ff6-400e-5858-be77-8608b194d8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.0lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228348} {:answer/id #uuid "37221632-d09d-5e81-8be5-0ac427f473d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.5lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228349} {:answer/id #uuid "719abf85-d7e7-5fed-8da5-1c3347fb8a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.2lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228350}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d74abd8b-f96b-59a5-a0ea-b2c09890154a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228345}] :question/id #uuid "138b92a0-177f-5f9d-993c-5b85e485defc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336499" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $210gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $120gal$ of nutrient solution with $30lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66750a16-700f-5c23-a55a-30496608f331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377200} {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67e761-f48e-5810-bebf-b6e7784e791c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377202} {:answer/id #uuid "0facc8a8-1164-510f-a7f4-a0e94be929e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377203} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0c788-e151-5687-a6d5-ff813c78241e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7f9793-5f2d-5fa5-9828-29145c21e291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2fb266c-0e36-5075-b402-7e3b0f1013d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336506" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $21\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=70+21t-14t=7(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10.5-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =10.5(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =10.5(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 10.5(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =10.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3.5(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-15 \\\\ C & =-15(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)-\\frac{15(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.5(10+t)-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 70+21{t}_{o}-14{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 70+7{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 7{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3.5(10+20)-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-15{\\left(\\frac{10}{30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈103.3\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d60df00e-8828-5ca8-b2ed-d8bd599eb07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377207} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa07fe2-d9eb-58e7-adb1-02efd7fc679e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377208} {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf4199-9fdf-5b11-9f3c-4a9a9611b20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377210} {:answer/id #uuid "e6800f5a-8ca4-5d4d-a314-681c9b7bdc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a930be-e052-5424-bf3a-7194b15d9c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377206}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a16983-dbf1-596b-b57d-9f38439fdd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336513" :question/problem "A flavoring tank at a beverage factory can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of flavoring solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved flavoring. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which flavoring enters the tank minus the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of flavoring enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *flavoring* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=75+9t-6t=3(25+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *flavoring* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(25+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|25+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(25+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q) & =(25+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (25+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(25+t)Q & =6(25+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2}) & =6(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(25+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(25+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(25+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(25+t)+\\frac{C}{(25+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of flavoring. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(25+0)+\\frac{C}{(25+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 50+\\frac{C}{(25)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{25}^{2}} & =-25 \\\\ C & =-25(25)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(25+t)-\\frac{25(25)^{2}}{(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(25+t)-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{25+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 75+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 75+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =75 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2(25+25)-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{25+25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =100-25{\\left(\\frac{25}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈93.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3931a-6dfb-5fbc-8b0f-dbd997b8f656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377214} {:answer/id #uuid "0562dc6c-24dc-5a4f-a3f1-00a900d6d777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377215} {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} {:answer/id #uuid "976b22bc-fda8-59e2-abbb-17c2352700de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377217} {:answer/id #uuid "a8deab91-7c4b-52d1-bbed-ff4091b43b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "159.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df70905-5278-5516-abae-b8ae4602e0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377213}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "729a2fd5-523f-5eb2-bf2f-20fedeba14f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336516" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $200\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =12(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =12(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 4(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 40+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-30 \\\\ C & =-30(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(10+t)-\\frac{30(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(10+t)-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $200\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=200$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =200 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(10+30)-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =160-30{\\left(\\frac{10}{40}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈158.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635d2341-3a33-5ac9-bc90-9aa868281876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385210} {:answer/id #uuid "923fae5f-f36e-59d7-a2f9-4b1a32154401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc28125-aa6b-5b7d-9978-9da8bc0bd171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385213} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd0d588-4274-52c3-9c9a-c8c45c106ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fe85108-505b-58b7-a19a-be78c15e1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212} :answer-field/id #uuid "711b7022-c824-5eae-90a8-e7d790b19008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c6933cf-ef37-5bf5-8816-b51b8e56e9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336501" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.6\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+15t-10t=5(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =9(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =9(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(20+t)-\\frac{40(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(20+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 100+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 100+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =3(20+16)-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+16}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =108-40{\\left(\\frac{20}{36}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈95.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} {:answer/id #uuid "7fddeb67-7d2e-5a6b-8751-43edc5489a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385218} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6744f-c23c-5448-817b-4573055d4ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "129.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385219} {:answer/id #uuid "e29bac7e-c5b7-5397-996e-06f067a62576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385220} {:answer/id #uuid "01805774-f2d7-5739-bed3-d2bf97e1dfc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fff374-e865-54f3-a150-c5efb630780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7d8e34-4951-5d39-86d9-5779f3f14045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d3bb996-2c5a-57e9-940a-6b48f2e05670" :question/math-academy-id "q-336502" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $160\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $100\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=100+6t-4t=2(50+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(50+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{50+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(50+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|50+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(50+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(50+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{50+t}\\,Q) & =(50+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (50+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(50+t)Q & =6(50+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2}) & =6(50+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(50+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(50+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(50+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(50+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(50+t)+\\frac{C}{(50+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(50+0)+\\frac{C}{(50+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 100+\\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(50)^{2}} & =-75 \\\\ C & =-75(50)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(50+t)-\\frac{75(50)^{2}}{(50+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(50+t)-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{50+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $160\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=160$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =160 \\\\ 100+6{t}_{o}-4{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 100+2{t}_{o} & =160 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =2(50+30)-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{50+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =160-75{\\left(\\frac{50}{80}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈130.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60afaad7-5c74-5ffe-abde-22de9074b994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501645} {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd631b-b970-5565-be2b-b669ed3ae06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "189.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501727} {:answer/id #uuid "cc782d2e-ff11-5ac5-a0c7-6ccf48af546c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501874} {:answer/id #uuid "e50bcdd8-481a-5a75-a0f4-6d4a10f0bb53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727ecad7-0669-59e4-acfa-630e5b405f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "190.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501675} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eaf0f19-6003-5585-a879-f271b5fc12a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336519" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{20+30}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{\\left(\\frac{20}{50}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0112f1fc-6de5-513e-87f9-3b66ed92fd30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501465} {:answer/id #uuid "26fdd352-98f8-5049-9bca-4712271dc3ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501536} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc4d99a-a70c-5a49-aae0-f3604e67d27f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501638} {:answer/id #uuid "c69e4261-0557-5f97-9e81-25b3bb4c21af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501861} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1dcaab2-0d10-522e-bd0f-9e9fdbc1a915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501952} :answer-field/id #uuid "507a90fb-d7f1-59f5-8df1-dc619644c3da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502000}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336503" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{15+20}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{\\left(\\frac{15}{35}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b62b53-1b76-5884-8cf6-e623e6a2f279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539394} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} {:answer/id #uuid "49ad23b9-5753-5821-bf10-fcf46d63b245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539414} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0087ee-6487-5333-81b9-0c1398c381ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539416} {:answer/id #uuid "8e62ca35-4a8a-52bf-a4f8-7c4ea42efb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} :answer-field/id #uuid "b807e3c0-f519-5770-bffe-d3aeea20ea64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a0728d4-a0d3-515f-805e-eb1a7d3c019c" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+25t-20t=5(12+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12.5-\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q=12.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(12+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{4}\\cdot 12.5 \\\\ (12+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(12+t)^{3}Q & =12.5(12+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4}) & =12.5(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 12.5(12+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{4} & =12.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(12+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 2.5(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{(12)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{4}} & =-20 \\\\ C & =-20(12)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)-\\frac{20(12)^{4}}{(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =2.5(12+t)-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+t}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 60+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 60+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =2.5(12+24)-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{12+24}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =90-20{\\left(\\frac{12}{36}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈89.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} {:answer/id #uuid "69907d51-3bf1-5b65-bbb2-ae831146ccb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "222.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539422} {:answer/id #uuid "872c8957-d06f-5407-86a5-616dbaa82761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539430} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcf795-dfb5-51ba-9ea4-76d781abc0a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "246.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539450} {:answer/id #uuid "e82e736f-6d11-5a2d-83f2-a27bd4f7db0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f10b853-d565-5f3f-8bc1-e376bfffca18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21bb65fc-94da-5d56-9b2a-2fe8f762fdb9" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $368\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =16\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+20t-12t=8(37.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q=16.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(37.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|37.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(37.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q) & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 16 \\\\ (37.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(37.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5}) & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 16(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(37.5+t)^{1.5} & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(37.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=160,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =160 \\\\ 6.4(37.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+0)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ 240+\\frac{C}{(37.5)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{37.5}^{1.5}} & =-80 \\\\ C & =-80(37.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)-\\frac{80(37.5)^{1.5}}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =6.4(37.5+t)-80{\\left(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+t}\\right)}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $368\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=368$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =368 \\\\ 300+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 300+8{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =68 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =8.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8.5) & =6.4(37.5+8.5)-80{\\left(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+8.5}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & =294.4-80{\\left(\\frac{37.5}{46}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈235.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa09e5-f47d-5621-9398-dc8978b0d87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539393} {:answer/id #uuid "3c471766-6b4e-5806-89be-2166ca930256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539409} {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a76597-8ded-5bde-a591-93a2815617ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539443} {:answer/id #uuid "d57c8381-a0fe-5352-bcb4-fdc5674d137c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "265.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68e3566-38f2-5496-a173-8df05cbc7a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539441}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a39de0b-a8fb-57f4-97a8-3f35b38b481a" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $492\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=213+15t-9t=6(35.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10-\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q=10.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(35.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|35.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(35.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q) & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 10 \\\\ (35.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(35.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5}) & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 10(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(35.5+t)^{1.5} & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(35.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{142}{3},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 4(35.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+0)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 142+\\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =-\\frac{284}{3} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{284}{3}{\\left(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+t}\\right)}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $492\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=492$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =492 \\\\ 213+15{t}_{o}-9{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 213+6{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =279 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =46.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(46.5) & =4(35.5+46.5)-\\frac{284}{3}{\\left(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+46.5}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & =328-\\frac{284}{3}{\\left(\\frac{35.5}{82}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈301.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b4b74e-a664-52ce-a195-21a3d605fb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539411} {:answer/id #uuid "71401fcb-b5f8-5b4c-8bf5-f003372af32c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539424} {:answer/id #uuid "989c22ac-092f-5f6b-a7b9-e236e2ba3d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539434} {:answer/id #uuid "a796dcf6-c12a-5697-9493-d0728388bc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539437} {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f5cfc2-660c-58a7-af4f-432fa13dacdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539402}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6991e06d-da9f-57bf-8c9b-3cc0e133e514" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $80\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=32+8t-6t=2(16+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(16+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q) & =(16+t)^{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\ (16+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(16+t)^{2}Q & =4(16+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3}) & =4(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 4(16+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16+t)^{3} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(16+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16+t)+\\frac{C}{(16+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ 1(16+0)+\\frac{C}{(16+0)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =-12 \\\\ C & =-12(16)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16+t)-\\frac{12(16)^{3}}{(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =1(16+t)-12{\\left(\\frac{16}{16+t}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $80\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=80$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =80 \\\\ 32+8{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 32+2{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =48 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =1(16+24)-12{\\left(\\frac{16}{16+24}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =40-12{\\left(\\frac{16}{40}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈39.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1accc569-7a13-58f0-bb4e-fe4d837666e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539399} {:answer/id #uuid "4c421d3c-a021-523f-b9ba-63f0c8410839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539415} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aad8df-e1a6-5036-96dc-1c9eea79d2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539449} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ac5ae2-38a9-5b58-b64a-b2b42879cc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03249a1-4a5a-5426-a9d8-a51d09a0bcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7375207b-1d18-5bc8-88aa-4e960cad1491" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $82\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $3\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=33+3t-1t=2(16.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(1\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(1\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(16.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q) & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (16.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(16.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5}) & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16.5+t)^{0.5} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(16.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{33}{8},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 1(16.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+0)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 16.5+\\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =-\\frac{99}{8} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{99}{8}{\\left(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+t}\\right)}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $82\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=82$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =82 \\\\ 33+3{t}_{o}-1{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 33+2{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =49 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24.5) & =1(16.5+24.5)-\\frac{99}{8}{\\left(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+24.5}\\right)}^{0.5} \\\\  & =41-\\frac{99}{8}{\\left(\\frac{16.5}{41}\\right)}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈33.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19329e8b-f3ba-516f-af71-d4b1d311b395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "233.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539396} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebc62f3-c951-51bd-a0c0-b13a63587c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "193.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539410} {:answer/id #uuid "8e496fc1-51c0-50fa-b05e-7b8545cfbf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539432} {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} {:answer/id #uuid "fed2300e-a029-550d-98c0-c43563de994e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "209.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a6811d-be8c-5fac-8293-80fb147bef39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7b8493b-92bf-5e7a-80a8-38bdcc3f94e7" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $608gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $276gal$ of nutrient solution with $36.8lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.4lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $20gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.4\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $276\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=276+20t-12t=8(34.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q=8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(34.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|34.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(34.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q) & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 8 \\\\ (34.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(34.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5}) & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 8(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(34.5+t)^{1.5} & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(34.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $36.8\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=36.8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =36.8 \\\\ 3.2(34.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+0)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ 110.4+\\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =-73.6 \\\\ C & =-73.6(34.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)-\\frac{73.6(34.5)^{1.5}}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =3.2(34.5+t)-73.6{\\left(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+t}\\right)}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $608\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=608$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =608 \\\\ 276+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 276+8{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =332 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =41.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(41.5) & =3.2(34.5+41.5)-73.6{\\left(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+41.5}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & =243.2-73.6{\\left(\\frac{34.5}{76}\\right)}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈220.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} {:answer/id #uuid "3070ff1a-7364-514c-8463-27955639a9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539404} {:answer/id #uuid "c36c1a8a-ce2f-5284-b361-d99daa4298d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539445} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bef1a7-bd82-52c0-a027-9770a4051e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "289.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539446} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f09433-dc8c-51ad-bba5-d5f7876a82dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cc63e8-6dde-540e-9e8a-53412c30e26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539429}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd6eb638-b03a-52f5-961d-00f47eebd890" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $290\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=110+25t-20t=5(22+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =25-\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q=25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(22+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|22+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(22+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(22+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q) & =(22+t)^{4}\\cdot 25 \\\\ (22+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(22+t)^{3}Q & =25(22+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4}) & =25(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 25(22+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(22+t)^{4} & =25\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(22+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(22+t)+\\frac{C}{(22+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=55,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =55 \\\\ 5(22+0)+\\frac{C}{(22+0)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ 110+\\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =-55 \\\\ C & =-55(22)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(22+t)-\\frac{55(22)^{4}}{(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =5(22+t)-55{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+t}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $290\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=290$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =290 \\\\ 110+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 110+5{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =36\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(36) & =5(22+36)-55{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+36}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =290-55{\\left(\\frac{22}{58}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈288.9\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33657fab-2d53-58a0-98a5-5356a1b0e53a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539405} {:answer/id #uuid "481acaa8-6906-5974-ae76-54c5df18a291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539412} {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d6b5ed-3b6b-5ab9-afd8-dc8db825fe1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539436} {:answer/id #uuid "ecee97e7-7992-52dc-9300-131e135e7abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6be6c4b-dcbc-5ac0-a692-8e76768f9b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df8520ff-a1e4-578f-8153-7bfeb8954209" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $342gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $117gal$ of nutrient solution with $39lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $45gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $36gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $45\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $117\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $45\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $36\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=117+45t-36t=9(13+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $36\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(36\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =30-\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q=30.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(13+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|13+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(13+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(13+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q) & =(13+t)^{4}\\cdot 30 \\\\ (13+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(13+t)^{3}Q & =30(13+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4}) & =30(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 30(13+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(13+t)^{4} & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(13+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(13+t)+\\frac{C}{(13+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $39\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=39,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =39 \\\\ 6(13+0)+\\frac{C}{(13+0)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ 78+\\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(13)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(13+t)-\\frac{39(13)^{4}}{(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =6(13+t)-39{\\left(\\frac{13}{13+t}\\right)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $342\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=342$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =342 \\\\ 117+45{t}_{o}-36{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 117+9{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 9{t}_{o} & =225 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =6(13+25)-39{\\left(\\frac{13}{13+25}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =228-39{\\left(\\frac{13}{38}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈227.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59522728-6ff5-53b4-81e0-6fc27377f260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "139.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539419} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc28afa-a913-5db8-b93f-4620f9101e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539421} {:answer/id #uuid "7393c7e7-e7db-5dae-8dbd-5c124fce7375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539426} {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} {:answer/id #uuid "f63a9d0e-447c-57d4-a11e-5dba82466c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} :answer-field/id #uuid "5063c8db-660b-5ca6-90ed-32587c3a7e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539418}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbffe068-df49-5949-a93c-dd6aa67cd689" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $306\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=234+24t-18t=6(39+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(39+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|39+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(39+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(39+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q) & =(39+t)^{3}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (39+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(39+t)^{2}Q & =12(39+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3}) & =12(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 12(39+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(39+t)^{3} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(39+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(39+t)+\\frac{C}{(39+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=78,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =78 \\\\ 3(39+0)+\\frac{C}{(39+0)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ 117+\\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(39)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(39+t)-\\frac{39(39)^{3}}{(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =3(39+t)-39{\\left(\\frac{39}{39+t}\\right)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $306\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=306$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =306 \\\\ 234+24{t}_{o}-18{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 234+6{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =72 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =12\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =3(39+12)-39{\\left(\\frac{39}{39+12}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =153-39{\\left(\\frac{39}{51}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & ≈135.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf04547-57c3-53c4-840a-33e618532e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "284.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539403} {:answer/id #uuid "5acc0bd1-19cd-5dac-ac79-38ebff3903c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "588.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539420} {:answer/id #uuid "797eee29-2209-55c5-906c-e8f2d054d896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "458.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539427} {:answer/id #uuid "a62cbaf9-65b9-5cb1-860f-98a4438ffabe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "273.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} :answer-field/id #uuid "f243c2a4-f8d5-5f1f-93bf-3f804ba469a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539457}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff0fe97f-83da-58e7-9a2a-1f41369f6103" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $490\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=315+15t-5t=10(31.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(31.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|31.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(31.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q) & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (31.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(31.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5}) & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 18(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(31.5+t)^{0.5} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(31.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=126,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =126 \\\\ 12(31.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+0)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ 378+\\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =-252 \\\\ C & =-252(31.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)-\\frac{252(31.5)^{0.5}}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =12(31.5+t)-252{\\left(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+t}\\right)}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $490\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=490$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =490 \\\\ 315+15{t}_{o}-5{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 315+10{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =175 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =17.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(17.5) & =12(31.5+17.5)-252{\\left(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+17.5}\\right)}^{0.5} \\\\  & =588-252{\\left(\\frac{31.5}{49}\\right)}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈386.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} {:answer/id #uuid "56903a87-08bb-5210-b21a-15bdc9bdba3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540671} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb90bb5-f5d6-5864-b41d-7da56b21a5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540720} {:answer/id #uuid "bba99957-7e8d-5791-b9fd-9c58160a4cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540843} {:answer/id #uuid "c0463d14-2b9e-5fba-b2c4-9802c27cf249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} :answer-field/id #uuid "464a658d-9140-58a9-ae22-53b8f7f33f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a65a87-c8d0-5bbf-adbe-0789f4e08164" :question/math-academy-id "q-336500" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank at a bottling plant can hold up to $220\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of syrup solution with $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated sugar solution with concentration $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8.4-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=8.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 8.4 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =8.4(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =8.4(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 8.4(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =8.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ 2.8(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ 84+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-60 \\\\ C & =-60(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)-\\frac{60(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2.8(30+t)-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $220\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=220$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =220 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2.8(30+25)-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+25}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =154-60{\\left(\\frac{30}{55}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈136.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} {:answer/id #uuid "52c67f33-11db-51b1-9650-af1cf2900407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20928a6-6ffa-5ddc-9782-9f840a1c74b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540798} {:answer/id #uuid "aca174a9-712f-5452-958f-e15b38456d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540818} {:answer/id #uuid "d7682d19-2baf-59af-84e2-dbac5b3c49b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e476d3-efaf-5ec7-9cea-2c3e7d2d49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c55e9d5-5244-5391-84ab-9850b2ba8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-336508" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment tank at a chemical plant can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater with $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. A more concentrated wastewater stream with concentration $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =15\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =5(30+15)-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+15}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =225-110{\\left(\\frac{30}{45}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈176.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}]}
